{"reply":"You have a real trade-off here.

Putting all the extra on the credit card fits Mara’s wish to clear it fast. Splitting it fits Jun’s wish to keep building the emergency fund sooner.

I’d weigh two things: the card is first in your avalanche order, and it is the most expensive debt to carry; but your emergency fund is still below your 3-month target. So this is a speed-versus-cash-buffer choice, not a right-versus-wrong one.","options":[{"label":"A: all extra on the card","what_if":"debt1-W1","summary":"Put the full ₱9,050.00 monthly surplus to the credit card. The card is paid off in 9 months, with credit-card interest at ₱12,460.10. The car loan then finishes in 24 months.","sources":["figure:debt1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card","figure:debt1-W1:debt.total_interest@debt:credit-card","figure:debt1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:car-loan","fact:preference.debt_strategy","said"],"specialists":["debt"]},{"label":"B: Jun’s split","what_if":"savings1-W2","summary":"Put ₱4,000.00 extra a month to the credit card and the rest to the emergency fund. The emergency fund reaches its target in 12 months, and the credit card is paid off in 17 months.","sources":["figure:savings1-W2:savings.months_to_target","figure:savings1-W2:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card","figure:savings1-W2:cashflow.savings_rate","fact:household.emergency_months_target","said"],"specialists":["savings","debt"]}],"recommended":""}
